The Turbine is atleast a 5x7x5 Machine but can be expanded to hold more Steam and make more Energy! 

The frame of the Turbine is made of Turbine Casings. The Floor and Ceiling can be filled in with Turbine Casings or Structural Glass. 

Before we worry about the Walls let's focus on the inside! That's the real Money-maker! 

In the dead center from the Floor is where our Rotors will be placed. For the smallest one we'll need 2! Then, attach the Blades on them, 4 in total. 

Above the highest Rotor place our Rotational Complex and surround it with Pressure Dispersers. 

The Layer above the Rotational Complex will be Electromagnetic Coils and above them will be our Saturating Condensers. 

Now we can put our Ports and Vents in our Walls and fill the rest with Turbine Casings or Structural Glass!
{@pagebreak}
Thankfully the Turbine is much easier to use than Build! 

Tube in some Steam through the Port and it should start spinning. The amount of Steam it can hold is determined by the size of the entire Turbine Build. 

Then the Rotors and Blades will start spinning because of the Steam rising through the Turbine. This will move the Rotational Complex which moves the Electromagnetic Coils making Energy! 

The Energy can be Cabled out of our 2nd Port. The amount of Energy is determined on how fast the Rotors and Blades are moving. Which itself is determined on how much Steam is Input into the Turbine. 

If we use Saturating Condensers we'll get a little Water back from our Steam. We can take this Water out from the Vents and back into our Fission Reactor! 

We do have a setting within our Turbine GUI which is similar to those within the Electrolytic Separator. We can choose to Dump Excess Steam, to constantly have it working at fastest Energy production. We can Dump Steam, incase you want to get rid of it I guess... or Idle which is default! If you use one that isn't Idle, it won't recycle Water from Steam.
{@pagebreak}
This Multiblock can be made at any size available, not just what is shown through the Images. The Images merely show the smallest size we can make.
